,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":778.905018857,"data":"--- [algebra.sequences.linear_recurrence] ---\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":778.905020016,"data":"Triggers: a_{n+1}=f(n)-k*a_n, monotonicity constraint, closed form threshold\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":778.90502382,"data":"Technique: Linear recurrence a_{n+1}=2^n-7a_n with monotonicity constraint. Find minimal 1/a_0 such that a_{n+1}\u003ea_n for all positive n. The recurrence has closed form; the constraint becomes an inequality on a_0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":778.905032157,"data":"Question: A sequence of real numbers {a_n} satisfies that: a_{n+1}=2^n-7a_n, n=0,1,2,... Find the minimal possible value of 1/a_0 such that a_{n+1}\u003ea_n for any positive integer n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":778.905033765,"data":"Answer: Solve the linear recurrence a_{n+1}=2^n-7a_n. The homogeneous solution is C*(-7)^n. The particular solution involves 2^n terms. Express a_n in terms of a_0, then impose a_{n+1}\u003ea_n for all n\u003e=1. This gives a threshold for a_0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":778.905035395,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":778.90503646,"data":"--- [algebra.factoring.simon_factoring_trick] ---\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":778.905037643,"data":"Triggers: Diophantine equation with cross term xy and linear terms, xy + ax + by = c, need integer solutions of bilinear equation\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":778.905038962,"data":"Technique: Simon's Favorite Factoring Trick (SFFT): xy + ax + by + ab = (x+b)(y+a). Add ab to both sides of xy+ax+by=c to get (x+b)(y+a)=c+ab. Then enumerate factor pairs of c+ab. Works for any bilinear Diophantine equation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":778.905040466,"data":"Question: Find all positive integer solutions to xy + 3x + 2y = 55.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":778.90504218,"data":"Answer: Add 6 to both sides: xy + 3x + 2y + 6 = 61. Factor: (x+2)(y+3) = 61. Since 61 is prime and x,y\u003e0: x+2\u003e2 and y+3\u003e3, so x+2=61, y+3=1 (impossible since y\u003e0) or x+2=1 (impossible since x\u003e0). Actually with positive integers: (x+2)(y+3)=61, factors of 61: 1*61 or 61*1. x+2=61=\u003ex=59,y+3=1=\u003ey=-2 (invalid). x+2=1=\u003ex=-1 (invalid). So no positive integer solutions. For non-prime RHS, enumerate all factor pairs.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":778.905044913,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":778.905046045,"data":"[END EXPERT NOTES]\n"}
